EXAM_DATA.papers["2003"] = {"id":"2003","year":2003,"label":"2003 旧卷","era":"old","sourceUrl":"https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/kaoyan/paper/2003/","timeLimitMin":180,"scoreMode":"raw","parts":[{"id":"section1","kind":"cloze","title":"Section I 完形填空","directions":"Directions: Read the following text. Choose the best word(s) for each numbered blank and mark A, B, C or D on the ANSWER SHEET . (10 points)","points":10,"passage":{"paragraphs":[{"id":"p-section1-1","text":"Teachers need to be aware of the emotional, intellectual, and physical changes that young adults experience. And they also need to give serious   {{1}}   to how they can best   {{2}}   such changes. Growing bodies need movement and   {{3}}   , but not just in ways that emphasize competition.   {{4}}   they are adjusting to their new bodies and a whole host of new intellectual and emotional challenges, teenagers are especially self-conscious and need the   {{5}}   that comes from achieving success and knowing that their accomplishments are   {{6}}   by others. However, the typical teenage lifestyle is already filled with so much competition that it would be   {{7}}   to plan activities in which there are more winners than losers,   {{8}}   , publishing newsletters with many student-written book reviews,   {{9}}   student artwork, and sponsoring book discussion clubs. A variety of small clubs can provide   {{10}}   opportunities for leadership, as well as for practice in successful   {{11}}   dynamics. Making friends is extremely important to teenagers, and many shy students need the   {{12}}   of some kind of organization with a supportive adult   {{13}}   visible in the background."},{"id":"p-section1-2","text":"In these activities, it is important to remember that the young teens have   {{14}}   attention spans. A variety of activities should be organized   {{15}}   participants can remain active as long as they want and then go on to   {{16}}   else without feeling guilty and without letting the other participants   {{17}}   . This does not mean that adults must accept irresponsibility.   {{18}}   , they can help students acquire a sense of commitment by   {{19}}   for roles that are within their   {{20}}   and their attention spans and by having clearly stated rules."}]},"questions":[{"n":1,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"thought"},{"key":"B","text":"idea"},{"key":"C","text":"opinion"},{"key":"D","text":"advice"}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"固定搭配","stemZh":"1.","reason":"give thought to 是固定搭配 线索就在本句本身：they also need to give serious __(1)__ to how they can best。give __ to sth 表示\"认真考虑某事\"时，习惯用 give thought to / give consideration to；give idea/opinion/advice to 在英语里并不是这种\"考虑\"含义的固定搭配。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"thought 与 give __ to 构成固定搭配 give thought to sth，意思正是\"思考、考虑\"，与前面 serious 一起就是\"认真考虑\"，正好接 how they can best __(2)__ such changes 这一思考对象，框架与语义都对得上。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"idea 译成\"想法\"看似通顺，但英语里没有 give serious idea to 这个搭配；表达\"有想法\"用 have an idea about，不进入 give __ to 的框架。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"opinion 指\"观点、意见\"，常用搭配是 give an opinion on/about\"对…发表意见\"，主语是教师对自己的方案\"发表意见\"在语义上别扭，更不能接 to + 疑问词从句表示思考对象。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"advice 指\"建议\"，固定搭配是 give advice to sb，对象是\"人\"，而本句空后接的是 to how they can best __(2)__ such changes 这样的\"事\"，且主语是教师自己在思考，不是在对外建议。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":2,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"strengthen"},{"key":"B","text":"accommodate"},{"key":"C","text":"stimulate"},{"key":"D","text":"enhance"}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"2.","reason":"开篇定调=回应、适应青少年的变化 线索回到首句 Teachers need to be aware of the emotional, intellectual, and physical changes that young adults experience——教师的姿态是\"察觉并回应\"这些既成的变化，下文整段都在讲怎么\"配合\"这些变化设计活动（让他们动、给他们成就感、避免过度竞争），所以该填的动词应有\"顺应、配合、接纳\"之义，而不是\"加强\"或\"刺激\"变化本身。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"strengthen 是\"加强、强化\"，与 changes 搭配会理解成\"加大青少年的身心动荡\"，与教师\"察觉并稳妥回应\"的姿态相反，语义指向错。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"accommodate 在此意为\"适应、迁就、为…作出安排\"，accommodate the changes 即\"针对这些变化作出相应的安排\"，与首句 be aware of …changes 一脉相承，也呼应下文\"设计多赢的活动\"。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"stimulate 是\"刺激、激励\"，对象通常是人或兴趣，对 changes 用 stimulate 会理解成\"再去刺激更多的变化发生\"，与作者\"稳妥回应\"的态度相悖。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"enhance 多用于\"提升、增强（质量、价值、效果）\"，色彩偏褒义，宾语常是 quality/effect/image；用于 changes 既色彩不合，也不是教师该做的事。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":3,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"care"},{"key":"B","text":"nutrition"},{"key":"C","text":"exercise"},{"key":"D","text":"leisure"}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"3.","reason":"but not just in ways that emphasize competition 锁定\"运动\" 线索是紧跟其后的 but not just in ways that emphasize competition——只有\"运动/锻炼\"才存在\"强调竞争与否\"的方式问题；care/nutrition/leisure 都不天然与 competition 形成对照。同时与 movement 并列也提示同属\"身体活动\"。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"care\"照料\"宽泛而抽象，无法解释为什么作者要紧接着谈\"不要只强调竞争的方式\"——care 不存在\"竞争式 vs 非竞争式\"之分。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"nutrition\"营养\"虽是\"成长中的身体\"所需，但与下半句 competition 完全接不上：没有\"以竞争方式提供营养\"这种说法。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"exercise\"锻炼\"与 movement 同属身体活动，正好可以\"有的方式强调竞争、有的方式不强调\"，无缝承接 but not just in ways that emphasize competition。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"leisure\"闲暇\"指空闲时间或休闲，不是\"成长中的身体所需\"的活动类项，且 leisure 与 competition 也对不上文意。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":4,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"If"},{"key":"B","text":"Although"},{"key":"C","text":"Whereas"},{"key":"D","text":"Because"}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"逻辑关系","stemZh":"4.","reason":"正在适应巨大变化→所以格外敏感，是因果 前半句 they are adjusting to their new bodies and a whole host of new intellectual and emotional challenges\"他们正在适应新身体与各种新挑战\"，后半句 teenagers are especially self-conscious\"青少年格外敏感、需要被认可\"——前句是后句的原因，这是顺承因果关系，不是\"虽然…但是\"。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"If 引导假设条件，可前半句 are adjusting 是对青春期青少年正在发生的事实，不是假设；用 If 会读出\"假如他们正在适应…\"的别扭语气。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"Although 要求前后是让步—主张的对立关系（虽然X，但Y与X相反），可\"正在适应巨大变化\"与\"格外敏感\"在方向上一致，不构成让步。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"Whereas 表对比，常用于两组对立主体（如\"A喜欢X，而B喜欢Y\"）；本句前后说的是同一主体的状态与反应，没有对比对象。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"Because 引出原因：正因为他们正在应对身体与心理上的巨变，所以才格外敏感、需要 the __(5)__ that comes from achieving success，因果链条紧实。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":5,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"assistance"},{"key":"B","text":"guidance"},{"key":"C","text":"confidence"},{"key":"D","text":"tolerance"}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"5.","reason":"来自成功与被认可的，是\"自信\" 线索是 that comes from achieving success and knowing that their accomplishments are __(6)__ by others——\"取得成功、被他人认可\"带来的心理收益，对应的就是 self-confidence/confidence\"自信\"。其他三项（援助/指导/容忍）的来源都不是\"自己的成就被认可\"。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"assistance\"援助\"通常 comes from sb else 而非 from achieving success；与 achieving success 不构成因果链。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"guidance 同样 comes from teachers/elders，不是 from achieving success；学生先有 guidance 才可能成功，顺序颠倒。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"confidence\"自信\"正是 from achieving success and knowing that their accomplishments are admired by others 这一过程的心理产物；也接续 self-conscious 的语境——越是敏感，越需要自信支撑。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"tolerance\"容忍/宽容\"既不是\"取得成功\"的结果，也不是\"被认可\"的结果，与定语从句完全脱节。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":6,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"claimed"},{"key":"B","text":"admired"},{"key":"C","text":"ignored"},{"key":"D","text":"surpassed"}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"6.","reason":"与 confidence、achieving success 同向，需褒义动词 前半句 the __(5)__ that comes from achieving success and knowing that their accomplishments are __(6)__ by others 把 confidence 与\"成就被他人 admire\"并列为青少年的心理需求来源，必须是褒义且对应\"被认可、被赞赏\"。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"claimed\"被声称/被索取\"——表示被人据为己有，与\"得到他人认可\"完全不同；如\"他人把青少年的成就据为己有\"反而是贬义。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"admired\"被赞赏\"正是\"自信\"的另一来源——知道自己的成绩被他人欣赏。色彩、对象、逻辑全部对齐。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"ignored\"被无视\"是负面体验，恰恰会摧毁 confidence，与作者主张相反。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"surpassed\"被超过\"意味着\"别人比他做得更好\"，是打击自信的体验，与 confidence comes from … 的链条相反。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":7,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"improper"},{"key":"B","text":"risky"},{"key":"C","text":"fair"},{"key":"D","text":"wise"}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"7.","reason":"前文用 However 已表对竞争过多的不满 本句以 However, the typical teenage lifestyle is already filled with so much competition 开头，作者明显认为现有竞争已经过多；紧接着提出\"多设赢家的活动\"——这是作者推荐的方案，故 it would be X 中的 X 应当是褒义的评价词。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"improper\"不当的\"是负面评价，作者却是在推荐 more winners than losers，色彩相反。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"risky\"有风险的\"也是负面评价，与作者明确推荐 multiple opportunities 的态度矛盾。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"fair\"公平的\"恰恰意味着赢家输家比例对等；而活动里\"赢家多于输家\"本身是不\"公平\"竞争，用 fair 形容反而自相矛盾。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"wise\"明智的\"是褒义评价，与 However 之后作者\"少竞争、多成就\"的立场一致；it would be wise to plan… 即\"明智的做法是去策划…\"。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":8,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"in effect"},{"key":"B","text":"as a result"},{"key":"C","text":"for example"},{"key":"D","text":"in a sense"}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"逻辑关系","stemZh":"8.","reason":"后文三件事是\"多赢活动\"的具体示例 空后并列着 publishing newsletters with many student-written book reviews, __(9)__ student artwork, and sponsoring book discussion clubs 三件具体活动；它们正是前文 activities in which there are more winners than losers 的实例。这正是\"举例\"关系。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"in effect\"事实上、实际上\"用来揭示\"看似A，实际是B\"，后接的是对前句的再定性，而不是若干并列例子。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"as a result\"因此、结果\"需要前面是原因，后面是结果；可后面三个动名词是\"活动方式\"，不是\"结果\"。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"for example\"例如\"完美对应后面 publishing/__/sponsoring 三个并列动名词——它们就是\"赢家多于输家的活动\"的具体例子。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"in a sense\"在某种意义上\"是对前句的限定性补充说明，引出的是抽象的再阐释，而非具体活动清单。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":9,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"displaying"},{"key":"B","text":"describing"},{"key":"C","text":"creating"},{"key":"D","text":"exchanging"}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"9.","reason":"同侪是\"发表书评\"\"赞助读书会\"，目的都是让学生作品被看见 左右两件活动 publishing newsletters with many student-written book reviews 与 sponsoring book discussion clubs 都是\"让更多学生的作品/声音被看到、被参与\"，故对 student artwork 也应是\"让它被看到\"——展示。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"displaying\"展示\"——展览学生美术作品，与\"出版书评、组织读书会\"一样，都是让学生的成就被他人看到、感受 their accomplishments are __(6)__ by others 的认可。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"describing\"描述\"是言语行为，displaying 才是\"举办活动\"。\"描述学生作品\"在中文里像活动，但 describe 在英文里更接近\"口头介绍\"，不构成可让多人参与的活动。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"creating\"创作\"主语应是学生本人，而本句的主语隐含是教师/组织者；教师组织的活动是\"展示\"作品，而不是替学生\"创作\"。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"exchanging\"交换\"是私下作品互换，并不能让 their accomplishments are admired by others 的\"被他人认可\"目的实现。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":10,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"durable"},{"key":"B","text":"excessive"},{"key":"C","text":"surplus"},{"key":"D","text":"multiple"}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"10.","reason":"A variety of small clubs 已暗示\"多样\"是好事 本句主语就是 A variety of small clubs\"多样的小俱乐部\"，这是作者的推荐做法；与之对应的 opportunities 应是中性褒义\"多种多样的\"，而非\"过多的\"\"多余的\"。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"durable\"耐用的\"用于实物（durable goods/material），不修饰抽象的 opportunities。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"excessive\"过度的\"暗示\"机会多到不必要\"，与作者推荐\"多设机会\"的态度相反。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"surplus\"过剩的、多余的\"也带\"超出需求\"的意味，并非\"种类多\"。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"multiple\"多种多样的\"与 a variety of 同向，强调\"机会种类丰富\"，正合作者用小俱乐部提供\"多个\"领导机会的本意。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":11,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"group"},{"key":"B","text":"individual"},{"key":"C","text":"personnel"},{"key":"D","text":"corporation"}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"11.","reason":"上下文的 small clubs/leadership 已圈定\"团队\"语境 线索是同句 A variety of small clubs can provide __(10)__ opportunities for leadership：在小俱乐部里练领导力，本质就是练\"团队互动\"，与之相配的固定搭配是 group dynamics。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"group 与 dynamics 构成 group dynamics\"团体动力学/小组互动\"，正是俱乐部、读书会一类多人活动训练的核心。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"individual\"个人的\"与 dynamics 不构成固定术语，且 clubs/leadership 强调多人协作，individual 与之相反。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"personnel\"人事\"是商务/公司语境用词，俱乐部场景不会用 personnel dynamics 这种说法。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"corporation\"公司\"指法人实体，与青少年俱乐部的语境格格不入。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":12,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"consent"},{"key":"B","text":"insurance"},{"key":"C","text":"admission"},{"key":"D","text":"security"}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"12.","reason":"shy students 与 supportive adult 双线索 线索一是主语 many shy students\"害羞的学生\"——他们最需要的是\"安全感\"；线索二是后续的 a supportive adult __(13)__ visible in the background\"在背景里若隐若现支持的成人\"——\"支持\"的功能就是提供安全感，故空填 security。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"consent\"同意\"通常指 parental consent\"父母同意\"，与 shy students 的心理需要并非同一维度。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"insurance\"保险\"是经济保障术语，不形容心理上的被支持感。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"admission\"准入\"指进入资格，但害羞学生缺的不是\"被允许进\"，而是进去之后的安全感。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"security\"安全感\"既贴合 shy students 的心理需要，也贴合 a supportive adult 所能提供的氛围。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":13,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"particularly"},{"key":"B","text":"barely"},{"key":"C","text":"definitely"},{"key":"D","text":"rarely"}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"13.","reason":"in the background=不显眼，barely=几乎不 线索是 in the background\"在背景里\"——成人不冲到台前抢戏；与之同向的副词只能是表示\"几乎不、勉强\"的程度副词 barely。\"特别可见\"\"绝对可见\"与\"背景里\"矛盾，\"很少可见\"是频率副词，含义有偏差。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"particularly\"特别明显\"把成人推向显眼位置，与\"在背景里\"相反。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"barely\"几乎不\"+ visible=\"勉强可见\"，正是\"成人若隐若现地支持\"的姿态，给害羞学生留出 security 又不让其感到被监视。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"definitely\"绝对/确定\"+ visible 强调\"明明白白可见\"，与背景里相反。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"rarely\"很少\"是频率，描述\"频次少\"而非\"程度低\"；rarely visible 在英语里不自然，且暗含\"成人经常不在\"，与\"提供 security\"矛盾。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":14,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"similar"},{"key":"B","text":"long"},{"key":"C","text":"different"},{"key":"D","text":"short"}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"14.","reason":"下文要求活动多样、可随时切换→注意力短 线索是紧接其后的 A variety of activities should be organized __(15)__ participants can remain active as long as they want and then go on to __(16)__ else without feeling guilty——只有当青少年注意力\"短\"，才需要让他们\"想干多久干多久，然后换别的\"。这反推 attention spans 必须是 short。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"similar需要给出\"和谁相似\"，本句无比较对象，且对策\"可随时切换\"也不源于\"相似\"。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"long\"长\"的注意力意味着可以专注做一件事很久，无需\"灵活切换\"，与对策相反。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"different\"不同的\"只说差异，没解释为什么要安排多样活动；逻辑不闭合。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"short\"短\"的注意力，正好解释为什么活动要\"参与者想做多久做多久、然后换\"——避免他们呆坐。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":15,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"if only"},{"key":"B","text":"now that"},{"key":"C","text":"so that"},{"key":"D","text":"even if"}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"逻辑关系","stemZh":"15.","reason":"组织活动是为了让参与者灵活进出 线索来自主从句的关系：主句 A variety of activities should be organized 是手段，从句 participants can remain active as long as they want and then go on to __(16)__ else 是想要达到的状态，二者是\"为了…\"的目的关系。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"if only 通常引导虚拟愿望或唯一条件，与\"被组织以便\"的逻辑不匹配。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"now that\"既然\"引导既成事实作原因，但本句的从句\"参与者可以保持活跃\"是\"想要达到的目的\"，不是已发生的事实。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"so that\"以便\"+ can 引导目的从句：组织多样活动，以便参与者可以按需自由进出，完美咬合 short attention spans 的对策。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"even if 引导让步，需要前后有对立，可主从句方向一致，不存在让步。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":16,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"everything"},{"key":"B","text":"anything"},{"key":"C","text":"nothing"},{"key":"D","text":"something"}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"16.","reason":"短注意力→换一件别的事做 线索来自全句逻辑 remain active as long as they want and then go on to __(16)__ else without feeling guilty——做完一件，再去做\"某件别的\"事，自然是 something else。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"everything else 意思是\"所有别的事\"，与\"换一件做\"矛盾。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"anything else 在肯定句里偏\"任何/随便什么\"，过于开放，且语气不如 something 自然。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"nothing else=\"别的什么都不做\"，与活动安排让他们继续参与的初衷相反。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"something else\"别的某件事\"是最自然的搭配，承接\"做了 X，再去做 Y\" 的灵活切换语境。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":17,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"off"},{"key":"B","text":"down"},{"key":"C","text":"out"},{"key":"D","text":"alone"}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"固定搭配","stemZh":"17.","reason":"让别人失望=let sb down 线索来自主语动作 participants can remain active as long as they want and then go on to __(16)__ else——可以自由切换却又\"不让其他参与者失望\"，正是 let sb down\"使…失望\"的固定表达。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"off 与 let 组合是 let sb off\"放过某人/免于惩罚\"，与\"避免让别人失望\"无关。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"down 与 let 组成 let sb down\"使某人失望\"，正合\"不能让其他参与者失望\"的本意。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"out 是\"放出去/泄露\"，与本句\"不让别人失望\"无关。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"alone 与 let 组成 let alone 是\"更不用说\"，固定用于负向并列，跟本句结构完全不符。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":18,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"On the contrary"},{"key":"B","text":"On the average"},{"key":"C","text":"On the whole"},{"key":"D","text":"On the other hand"}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"逻辑关系","stemZh":"18.","reason":"前句否定→后句给出相反主张 前句 This does not mean that adults must accept irresponsibility\"这并不意味着成人必须接受不负责任\"已经否定一种误解；后句 they can help students acquire a sense of commitment\"反而能帮助学生培养责任感\"立的是相反主张。这就是 on the contrary\"恰恰相反\"的标志结构。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"On the contrary\"恰恰相反\"——并非接受不负责，相反，要帮学生养成责任感，与前句否定结构完美对接。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"On the average\"平均来说\"用于数据/概率语境，与本句的态度反转无关。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"On the whole\"总的来说\"用来概括，不引出立场反转。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"On the other hand 通常配 On the one hand 使用，引出对照另一面，而非\"恰恰相反\"的正面反转。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":19,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"making"},{"key":"B","text":"standing"},{"key":"C","text":"planning"},{"key":"D","text":"taking"}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"固定搭配","stemZh":"19.","reason":"为学生\"规划/筹划\"力所能及的角色 线索来自上下文 they can help students acquire a sense of commitment by __(19)__ for roles that are within their __(20)__ and their attention spans——成人帮助学生承担\"力所能及的角色\"，对应的动作是 plan for\"为…作规划\"。其他三项要么意义不对，要么搭配不当。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"making 与 for 组成 make for 是\"走向/导致\"，与\"为角色作安排\"语义对不上。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"standing 与 for 组成 stand for 是\"代表、支持\"，对象通常是抽象理念，不接\"角色\"。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"planning for roles\"为这些角色作规划\"，即成人为学生筹划合适的角色，正是培养责任感的手段。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"taking 与 for 组成 take for 多半是\"把…误认作\"（take A for B），不是\"为角色筹划\"。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":20,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"capability"},{"key":"B","text":"responsibility"},{"key":"C","text":"proficiency"},{"key":"D","text":"efficiency"}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"20.","reason":"与 attention spans 并列，表\"能力范围\" 线索是并列项 and their attention spans\"注意力范围\"——attention spans 是青少年的某种\"容量边界\"，与之并列的也应是个体能力范围，capability\"能力\"恰好对应；其他三项要么程度过高（proficiency=熟练程度），要么对象错（responsibility=责任，本就是要培养的，不能成为\"边界\"），要么场景错（efficiency=效率）。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"capability\"能力\"指可承担事务的个人容量，within their capability\"在他们能力范围内\"，与 attention spans 并列共指\"个人天然容量\"，且对应上文\"成人帮助学生承担与其能力匹配的角色\"。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"responsibility\"责任\"在前文 a sense of commitment 里正是要培养的目标，不能反过来当\"角色应处于的范围\"——逻辑循环。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"proficiency\"熟练程度\"指对某技能的高水平掌握，要求过高；让青少年只承担\"熟练范围内\"的角色不切实际。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"efficiency\"效率\"指做事单位投入产出，不构成\"个人容量边界\"，且 within their efficiency 不是地道搭配。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}}]},{"id":"section2-part-a-1","kind":"reading","title":"Text 1","textNo":1,"directions":"Directions: Read the following four texts. Answer the questions after each text by choosing A, B, C or D. Mark your answers on the ANSWER SHEET . (40 points)","points":10,"passage":{"title":"Text 1","paragraphs":[{"id":"p-section2-part-a-1-1","text":"Wild Bill Donovan would have loved the Internet. The American spymaster who built the Office of Strategic Services in the World War Ⅱ and later laid the roots for the CIA was fascinated with information. Donovan believed in using whatever tools came to hand in the \"great game\" of espionage—spying as a \"profession.\" These days the Net, which has already re-made such everyday pastimes as buying books and sending mail, is reshaping Donovan's vocation as well."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-1-2","text":"The latest revolution isn't simply a matter of gentlemen reading other gentlemen's e-mail. That kind of electronic spying has been going on for decades. In the past three or four years, the World Wide Web has given birth to a whole industry of point-and-click spying. The spooks call it \"open source intelligence,\" and as the Net grows, it is becoming increasingly influential. In 1995 the CIA held a contest to see who could compile the most data about Burundi. The winner, by a large margin, was a tiny Virginia company called Open Source Solutions, whose clear advantage was its mastery of the electronic world."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-1-3","text":"Among the firms making the biggest splash in the new world is Straitford, Inc., a private intelligence-analysis firm based in Austin, Texas. Straitford makes money by selling the results of spying (covering nations from Chile to Russia) to corporations like energy-services firm McDermott International. Many of its predictions are available online at www.straitford.com."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-1-4","text":"Straitford president George Friedman says he sees the online world as a kind of mutually reinforcing tool for both information collection and distribution, a spymaster's dream. Last week his firm was busy vacuuming up data bits from the far corners of the world and predicting a crisis in Ukraine. \"As soon as that report runs, we'll suddenly get 500 new internet sign-ups from Ukraine,\" says Friedman, a former political science professor. \"And we'll hear back from some of them.\" Open-source spying does have its risks, of course, since it can be difficult to tell good information from bad. That's where Straitford earns its keep."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-1-5","text":"Friedman relies on a lean staff of 20 in Austin. Several of his staff members have military-intelligence backgrounds. He sees the firm's outsider status as the key to its success. Straitford's briefs don't sound like the usual Washington back-and-forthing, whereby agencies avoid dramatic declarations on the chance they might be wrong. Straitford, says Friedman, takes pride in its independent voice."}]},"questions":[{"n":21,"stem":"The emergence of the Net has____.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"received support from fans like Donovan"},{"key":"B","text":"remolded the intelligence services"},{"key":"C","text":"restored many common pastimes"},{"key":"D","text":"revived spying as a profession"}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"细节题","stemZh":"互联网的出现已经____。","choicesZh":{"A":"得到了像Donovan这样的支持者的支持","B":"重塑了情报机构","C":"恢复了许多常见的消遣活动","D":"使间谍活动作为一种职业得以复兴"},"analysis":{"type":"Q21 是一个待补全的陈述句 The emergence of the Net has____.，问\"互联网出现之后发生了什么客观结果\"，无 infer/suggest/imply 等推断标志，也不问主旨或态度。","parse":"The emergence of the Net has____. 是一个简单的现在完成时陈述句，主语为 The emergence of the Net，谓语 has 后面留空待补——空格里要填的是过去分词短语（动作的结果）。结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。","locateEn":"These days the Net, which has already re-made such everyday pastimes as buying books and sending mail, is reshaping Donovan's vocation as well.","locateZh":"如今互联网已经重塑了买书、寄信这类日常活动，如今它也在重塑 Donovan 的职业（情报业）。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"选项 received support from fans like Donovan 把因果颠倒——原文是\"Donovan 若还在世会喜欢互联网\"（虚拟语气 would have loved），并不是\"Donovan 真的支持了互联网\"；Donovan 早已不在世，更谈不上 received support。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"预判\"互联网重塑了情报业\"在 B 项里逐词找到对应：互联网（主语隐含）+ remolded（reshape）+ intelligence services（Donovan's vocation），无引申、无偏移，直接锁定 B。","pairs":[{"orig":"is reshaping Donovan's vocation","opt":"remolded the intelligence services"}]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"选项 restored many common pastimes 把 has already re-made such everyday pastimes as buying books and sending mail 的 re-made（重塑、改变形式）偷换成 restored（恢复、复原）——日常活动不是\"被恢复\"，而是\"被以新方式重塑\"。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"选项 revived spying as a profession 中的 revive（使复活、复兴）暗示\"间谍业曾衰落，现被互联网复活\"——原文从未说间谍业衰落过，反而强调它一直在 going on for decades；这是无中生有的引申。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":22,"stem":"Donovan's story is mentioned in the text to____.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"introduce the topic of online spying"},{"key":"B","text":"show how he fought for the US"},{"key":"C","text":"give an episode of the information war"},{"key":"D","text":"honor his unique services to the CIA"}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"例证目的题","stemZh":"文中提到Donovan的故事是为了____。","choicesZh":{"A":"引入网络间谍活动这一话题","B":"展示他如何为美国而战","C":"给出信息战的一个插曲","D":"表彰他为CIA提供的独特服务"},"analysis":{"type":"Donovan's story is mentioned in the text to____. 直接询问举这个例子\"是为了做什么\"——典型的例证目的题题干结构。","parse":"Donovan's story is mentioned in the text to____. 是被动句加不定式表目的的结构——主语 Donovan's story，谓语 is mentioned，不定式 to____ 表\"提及它的目的\"。结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。","locateEn":"Wild Bill Donovan would have loved the Internet. ... These days the Net, which has already re-made such everyday pastimes as buying books and sending mail, is reshaping Donovan's vocation as well.","locateZh":"Wild Bill Donovan 要是还在世会很喜欢互联网……如今互联网在重塑买书、寄信等日常活动之外，也在重塑 Donovan 的职业（情报业）。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"预判\"引出互联网与情报业话题\"在 A 项中 introduce the topic of online spying 一字对位；其余选项都没把 Donovan 当作\"引子\"，而是当作\"主角\"。","pairs":[{"orig":"These days the Net, which has already re-made such everyday pastimes as buying books and sending mail, is reshaping Donovan's vocation as well.","opt":"introduce the topic of online spying"}]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"选项 show how he fought for the US 把 Donovan 当作军事英雄来写，但原文只用 built the Office of Strategic Services in the World War Ⅱ and later laid the roots for the CIA 交代背景，没有任何\"如何作战\"的描写，更不是写他的传记。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"选项 give an episode of the information war 把原文的 was fascinated with information 偷换成 information war（信息战）——文中谈的是 espionage/spying（间谍/情报），不是国家间的\"信息战\"；Donovan 也不是一段\"信息战的插曲\"。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"选项 honor his unique services to the CIA 把段意夸大为\"颂扬他对 CIA 的独特贡献\"，但原文只说他 laid the roots for the CIA（为 CIA 奠定了根基），并未做颂扬性表态，本文整体也是新闻报道、不是悼词。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":23,"stem":"The phrase \"making the biggest splash\" (line 1, paragraph 3) most probably means____.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"causing the biggest trouble"},{"key":"B","text":"exerting the greatest effort"},{"key":"C","text":"achieving the greatest success"},{"key":"D","text":"enjoying the widest popularity"}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"词义句意题","stemZh":"短语\"making the biggest splash\"(第3段第1行)最可能的意思是____。","choicesZh":{"A":"引起最大的麻烦","B":"付出最大的努力","C":"取得最大的成功","D":"享有最广泛的欢迎"},"analysis":{"type":"The phrase \"making the biggest splash\" (line 1, paragraph 3) most probably means____. 直接问\"该短语最可能意为\"——词义句意题的标志性结构。","parse":"The phrase \"making the biggest splash\" (line 1, paragraph 3) most probably means____. 主语是被引号包裹的短语，谓语 means 后面接选项作宾语补足；line 1, paragraph 3 是括号插入语，提供定位坐标。结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。","locateEn":"Among the firms making the biggest splash in the new world is Straitford, Inc., a private intelligence-analysis firm based in Austin, Texas. Straitford makes money by selling the results of spying (covering nations from Chile to Russia) to corporations like energy-services firm McDermott International.","locateZh":"在这个新世界里影响最大的公司之中，就有 Straitford 公司——一家总部位于得州奥斯汀的私人情报分析公司。它靠把情报成果（覆盖从智利到俄罗斯）卖给 McDermott International 这类能源服务公司赚钱。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"选项 causing the biggest trouble 把短语理解为\"惹出最大的麻烦\"——与定位段对 Straitford 的全正面描述（赚钱、客户众多、预测在线可读）背道而驰。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"选项 exerting the greatest effort 描述的是\"付出最大努力\"（过程）——而 making the biggest splash 强调的是\"造成最大反响\"（结果）；原文也只描述 Straitford 的影响力大小，没说它付出了多少努力。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"本题正面极性已被定位段的赚钱、广泛覆盖客户、网站受访问等正面描述锁死；A 是负面、B、D 也不与正面极性等值，只有 C 在极性与语义\"最成功\"两点上都吻合。","pairs":[{"orig":"making the biggest splash in the new world","opt":"achieving the greatest success"}]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"选项 enjoying the widest popularity 把短语理解为\"最受欢迎\"——但 splash 强调的是\"影响/震动\"，不是\"受欢迎程度\"；而且 Straitford 是 B2B 公司（卖给 corporations），并非大众产品，更谈不上\"最广泛流行\"。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":24,"stem":"It can be learned from paragraph 4 that____.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"straitford's prediction about Ukraine has proved true"},{"key":"B","text":"straitford guarantees the truthfulness of its information"},{"key":"C","text":"straitford's business is characterized by unpredictability"},{"key":"D","text":"straitford is able to provide fairly reliable information"}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"推断题","stemZh":"从第4段可以得知____。","choicesZh":{"A":"Straitford关于乌克兰的预测已被证明是正确的","B":"Straitford保证其信息的真实性","C":"Straitford的业务特点是不可预测性","D":"Straitford能够提供相当可靠的信息"},"analysis":{"type":"It can be learned from paragraph 4 that____. 含 learned from，表明要从段内信息做一步合理引申——典型推断题题干。","parse":"It can be learned from paragraph 4 that____. 是 it 作形式主语 + that 从句的结构——真正的主语是 that 引导的从句，从句的内容由四个选项补全。结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。","locateEn":"Open-source spying does have its risks, of course, since it can be difficult to tell good information from bad. That's where Straitford earns its keep.","locateZh":"当然，开源情报也有它的风险，因为有时很难分辨信息的真伪。这恰恰就是 Straitford 安身立命之所在（即靠把好真伪关赚饭吃）。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"选项 straitford's prediction about Ukraine has proved true 用 has proved true（已被证实属实）强行盖棺——但原文只写 Straitford 在 predicting a crisis in Ukraine\"正在预测\"，并未给出该预测被证实的任何证据。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"选项 straitford guarantees the truthfulness of its information 把原文\"能较好地把真伪关\"夸大为\"担保信息的真实性\"——但原文先承认 does have its risks 风险存在，再说 Straitford 在这块见长；不可能反过来说它 guarantee（绝对担保）。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"选项 straitford's business is characterized by unpredictability 说 Straitford 的业务以\"不可预测性\"为特征——荒谬：Straitford 的本业就是 predicting（预测）！原文 predicting a crisis in Ukraine 恰好与 unpredictability 反义。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"预判\"Straitford 能提供较可靠信息\"在 D 项里几乎逐词对位（able to provide + fairly reliable + information）；fairly（相当）一词刚好回应原文 does have its risks 的让步，分寸感与原文一致，不像 B 项那样过头。","pairs":[{"orig":"That's where Straitford earns its keep.","opt":"straitford is able to provide fairly reliable information"}]}}}}},{"n":25,"stem":"Straitford is most proud of its____.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"official status"},{"key":"B","text":"nonconformist image"},{"key":"C","text":"efficient staff"},{"key":"D","text":"military background"}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"细节题","stemZh":"Straitford最引以为豪的是其____。","choicesZh":{"A":"官方地位","B":"不墨守成规的形象","C":"高效的员工","D":"军事背景"},"analysis":{"type":"Straitford is most proud of its____. 问的是 Straitford 一个客观属性，原文有明确表态句直接对应，不需要推断也不问主旨/态度。","parse":"Straitford is most proud of its____. 是\"主系表 + of 介词短语\"结构——主语 Straitford，系动词 is，表语 most proud of its____，空格里要填的是 its 后的名词性内容。结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。","locateEn":"He sees the firm's outsider status as the key to its success. Straitford's briefs don't sound like the usual Washington back-and-forthing, whereby agencies avoid dramatic declarations on the chance they might be wrong. Straitford, says Friedman, takes pride in its independent voice.","locateZh":"他认为公司\"局外人\"的身份是其成功的关键。Straitford 的简报不像华盛顿那些机构常见的来回打太极——那些机构生怕出错就不敢做出明确判断。Friedman 说，Straitford 以自己\"独立的声音\"为荣。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"选项 official status 中的 official（官方的）与原文 outsider status 直接反义——Straitford 引以为荣的恰恰是\"非官方/局外人\"身份，绝不可能是\"官方身份\"。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"预判\"独立/局外人形象\"在 B 项 nonconformist image 中找到精确对应，且原文有 outsider/independent 两处表述同时印证，证据链最强。","pairs":[{"orig":"outsider status","opt":"nonconformist image"},{"orig":"takes pride in its independent voice","opt":"nonconformist image"}]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"选项 efficient staff 借原文 Friedman relies on a lean staff of 20 in Austin. 制造干扰——lean staff 只说\"团队精干（人数少）\"，并未说\"高效\"；更关键的是，文中没有说 Straitford\"为之自豪\"的是它的员工。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"选项 military background 利用 Several of his staff members have military-intelligence backgrounds.——但原文只说\"几位成员有军情背景\"（Several），不是 Straitford 公司整体的特征，更不是它\"为之自豪\"的东西。","pairs":[]}}}}}]},{"id":"section2-part-a-2","kind":"reading","title":"Text 2","textNo":2,"directions":"","points":10,"passage":{"title":"Text 2","paragraphs":[{"id":"p-section2-part-a-2-1","text":"To paraphrase 18th-century statesman Edmund Burke, \"all that is needed for the triumph of a misguided cause is that good people do nothing.\" One such cause now seeks to end biomedical research because of the theory that animals have rights ruling out their use in research. Scientists need to respond forcefully to animal rights advocates, whose arguments are confusing the public and thereby threatening advances in health knowledge and care. Leaders of the animal rights movement target biomedical research because it depends on public funding, and few people understand the process of health care research. Hearing allegations of cruelty to animals in research settings, many are perplexed that anyone would deliberately harm an animal."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-2-2","text":"For example, a grandmotherly woman staffing an animal rights booth at a recent street fair was distributing a brochure that encouraged readers not to use anything that comes from or is tested in animals—no meat, no fur, no medicines. Asked if she opposed immunizations, she wanted to know if vaccines come from animal research. When assured that they do, she replied, \"Then I would have to say yes.\" Asked what will happen when epidemics return, she said, \"Don't worry, scientists will find some way of using computers.\" Such well-meaning people just don't understand."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-2-3","text":"Scientists must communicate their message to the public in a compassionate, understandable way—in human terms, not in the language of molecular biology. We need to make clear the connection between animal research and a grandmother's hip replacement, a father's bypass operation, a baby's vaccinations, and even a pet's shots. To those who are unaware that animal research was needed to produce these treatments, as well as new treatments and vaccines, animal research seems wasteful at best and cruel at worst."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-2-4","text":"Much can be done. Scientists could \"adopt\" middle school classes and present their own research. They should be quick to respond to letters to the editor, lest animal rights misinformation go unchallenged and acquire a deceptive appearance of truth. Research institutions could be opened to tours, to show that laboratory animals receive humane care. Finally, because the ultimate stakeholders are patients, the health research community should actively recruit to its cause not only well-known personalities such as Stephen Cooper, who has made courageous statements about the value of animal research, but all who receive medical treatment. If good people do nothing, there is a real possibility that an uninformed citizenry will extinguish the precious embers of medical progress."}]},"questions":[{"n":26,"stem":"The author begins his article with Edmund Burke's words to____.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"call on scientists to take some actions"},{"key":"B","text":"criticize the misguided cause of animal rights"},{"key":"C","text":"warn of the doom of biomedical research"},{"key":"D","text":"show the triumph of the animal rights movement"}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"例证目的题","stemZh":"作者在文章开头引用Edmund Burke的话是为了____。","choicesZh":{"A":"呼吁科学家采取一些行动","B":"批评动物权利这一被误导的事业","C":"警告生物医学研究的厄运","D":"展示动物权利运动的胜利"},"analysis":{"type":"题干用 to____ 的不定式追问作者引用 Edmund Burke 这句话的目的，典型的「为什么要举/引这个」结构。","parse":"主干 The author begins his article with Edmund Burke's words to____，介词短语 with Edmund Burke's words 修饰 begins，to 引导目的状语；空格处补一个动词短语，回答「为何引用」。结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。","locateEn":"To paraphrase 18th-century statesman Edmund Burke, \"all that is needed for the triumph of a misguided cause is that good people do nothing.\" One such cause now seeks to end biomedical research because of the theory that animals have rights ruling out their use in research. Scientists need to respond forcefully to animal rights advocates, whose arguments are confusing the public and thereby threatening advances in health knowledge and care.","locateZh":"套用 18 世纪政治家埃德蒙·伯克的话：「错误事业要得逞，所需要的只是好人无所作为。」当下就有这样一桩错误事业——试图以「动物有权利、不应被用于研究」为由终结生物医学研究。科学家必须有力回应动物权利倡导者，因为他们的言论正在误导公众，进而威胁健康知识与医疗的进步。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"预判结论「呼吁科学家行动」与 call on scientists to take some actions 在主体（scientists）、动作（take actions）两层都对得上，锁定 A。","pairs":[{"orig":"Scientists need to respond forcefully to animal rights advocates","opt":"call on scientists to take some actions"},{"orig":"all that is needed for the triumph of a misguided cause is that good people do nothing.","opt":"call on scientists to take some actions"}]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"criticize the misguided cause of animal rights 把作者对动物权利运动的批判（这是文章的话题背景）当成了引用 Burke 的目的；但引语的功能是「呼吁好人作为」，而非批判错误事业本身。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"warn of the doom of biomedical research 中的 doom（毁灭/末日）远比原文 threatening advances in health knowledge and care 来得严重；作者只是说「威胁进步」，并未断言生物医学注定毁灭。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"show the triumph of the animal rights movement 与原文事实正相反——原文是借 Burke 的话说「若好人不作为，错误事业才会得逞」，作者立场是要阻止其得逞，而非「展示其胜利」。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":27,"stem":"Misled people tend to think that using an animal in research is____.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"cruel but natural"},{"key":"B","text":"inhuman and unacceptable"},{"key":"C","text":"inevitable but vicious"},{"key":"D","text":"pointless and wasteful"}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"细节题","stemZh":"被误导的人们往往认为在研究中使用动物是____。","choicesZh":{"A":"残忍但自然","B":"不人道且不可接受","C":"不可避免但邪恶","D":"毫无意义且浪费"},"analysis":{"type":"Misled people tend to think that using an animal in research is____ 是要补出公众对动物实验的具体看法，属客观信息查找。","parse":"主语 Misled people，谓语 tend to think，that 引导宾语从句，从句主干是 using an animal in research is____，动名词短语作主语。结构无陷阱，但要意识到 Misled people 暗含「他们的看法不正确」的预设。","locateEn":"For example, a grandmotherly woman staffing an animal rights booth at a recent street fair was distributing a brochure that encouraged readers not to use anything that comes from or is tested in animals—no meat, no fur, no medicines.","locateZh":"例如，在最近一次街头集市上，一位慈眉善目的老太太守着动物权利宣传展位，散发的宣传册劝读者不要使用任何源自动物或经动物试验的东西——不吃肉、不穿皮草、不用药物。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"cruel but natural 中 cruel 抓对了一半，但 natural（自然/正常）暗含「虽然残忍，但可以接受」，而被误导者的态度恰恰是「不可接受」。语义极性后半段反了。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"B 项把「残忍」与「不可接受」两层都覆盖了，正好对应原文 cruel 与 wasteful 的双重判断，与预判一致。","pairs":[{"orig":"animal research seems wasteful at best and cruel at worst.","opt":"inhuman and unacceptable"}]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"inevitable but vicious 中 vicious（恶毒）勉强对应 cruel 一层，但 inevitable（不可避免）在原文找不到任何依据——原文是「他们认为这事是无谓的」，绝不会同时认为是「不可避免」。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"pointless and wasteful 两个词都对应 wasteful 一层（pointless≈wasteful），但完全没覆盖原文 cruel 的语义；用同义词把同一边重复了两次，反而丢了另一半。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":28,"stem":"The example of the grandmotherly woman is used to show the public's____.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"discontent with animal research"},{"key":"B","text":"ignorance about medical science"},{"key":"C","text":"indifference to epidemics"},{"key":"D","text":"anxiety about animal rights"}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"例证目的题","stemZh":"祖母般的妇女这一例子被用来展示公众的____。","choicesZh":{"A":"对动物研究的不满","B":"对医学科学的无知","C":"对流行病的漠不关心","D":"对动物权利的焦虑"},"analysis":{"type":"题干 The example of the grandmotherly woman is used to show 明确问例子的用途，是例证目的题的标准句式。","parse":"主语 The example of the grandmotherly woman，谓语 is used to show，宾语为 the public's____（名词的所有格 + 待补名词）。被动语态把作者藏起来，但作答方向不变——找作者用这例子要证明的论点。","locateEn":"Such well-meaning people just don't understand.","locateZh":"这些善意的人就是不懂（动物研究与医疗之间的关系）。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"discontent with animal research 「对动物研究不满」描述的更像作者眼中的对立面立场，而老妇人例子展示的核心问题是她不懂、不知情，而非她有理有据的不满。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"段末作者评价 just don't understand 与 ignorance about medical science 在动词名词化与上位词替换两个维度上严丝合缝，B 锁定。","pairs":[{"orig":"Such well-meaning people just don't understand.","opt":"ignorance about medical science"},{"orig":"Asked what will happen when epidemics return, she said, \"Don't worry, scientists will find some way of using computers.\"","opt":"ignorance about medical science"}]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"indifference to epidemics 抓的是老妇人那句「Don't worry」表面像「不在乎疫情」，但作者的论证重点不是她对疫情冷漠，而是她不懂没有动物研究就不会有疫苗这一医学常识。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"anxiety about animal rights 表达的是「为动物权利感到忧虑」，这是动物权利倡导者的立场，老妇人是被这种立场误导的公众一员，她本身并未表现出「对动物权利的焦虑」。把倡导者的特征强加给被误导的公众。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":29,"stem":"The author believes that, in face of the challenge from animal rights advocates, scientists should____.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"communicate more with the public"},{"key":"B","text":"employ hi-tech means in research"},{"key":"C","text":"feel no shame for their cause"},{"key":"D","text":"strive to develop new cures"}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"细节题","stemZh":"作者认为,面对动物权利倡导者的挑战,科学家应该____。","choicesZh":{"A":"更多地与公众沟通","B":"在研究中采用高科技手段","C":"对自己的事业感到无愧","D":"努力开发新的治疗方法"},"analysis":{"type":"The author believes that...scientists should____ 问的是作者明示的建议，对应原文一个具体表态句，属客观查找。","parse":"主语 The author，谓语 believes，that 宾语从句中 in face of the challenge from animal rights advocates 是状语，主干为 scientists should____。in face of 等于 facing，结构清晰无陷阱。","locateEn":"Scientists must communicate their message to the public in a compassionate, understandable way—in human terms, not in the language of molecular biology.","locateZh":"科学家必须以富有同理心、通俗易懂的方式与公众沟通——要说人话，而不是用分子生物学的术语。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"communicate 在原文与选项中字面一致，且第 3、4 段的整体论述都围绕「让公众听懂、参与」，与 A 完美贴合。","pairs":[{"orig":"Scientists must communicate their message to the public in a compassionate, understandable way","opt":"communicate more with the public"},{"orig":"Scientists could \"adopt\" middle school classes and present their own research.","opt":"communicate more with the public"}]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"employ hi-tech means in research 「在研究中使用高科技手段」其实是文中老妇人提到的 scientists will find some way of using computers. 的翻版，而那句话是作者举例嘲讽的对象，绝不是作者主张的对策。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"feel no shame for their cause 「不要为自己的事业感到羞耻」在原文无任何对应——作者全篇都没有说科学家曾经羞耻或应当克服羞耻，只字未提情感羞耻话题。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"strive to develop new cures 「努力研发新疗法」是科学家的本职工作，但题干问的是「面对动物权利倡导者挑战时科学家应当做什么」，作者的对策是沟通与发声，而非埋头做研究。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":30,"stem":"From the text we learn that Stephen Cooper is____.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"a well-known humanist"},{"key":"B","text":"a medical practitioner"},{"key":"C","text":"an enthusiast in animal rights"},{"key":"D","text":"a supporter of animal research"}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"细节题","stemZh":"从文中我们得知Stephen Cooper是____。","choicesZh":{"A":"一位知名的人道主义者","B":"一位医疗从业者","C":"一位动物权利热衷者","D":"一位动物研究的支持者"},"analysis":{"type":"From the text we learn that Stephen Cooper is____ 问的是文本对该人物身份/立场的直接说明，属信息查找。","parse":"主干 Stephen Cooper is____，From the text we learn that 是套语，无实义；唯一要注意的是别看到 well-known personalities 就自动联想到 famous/humanist，要严格扣作者给出的身份限定。","locateEn":"Finally, because the ultimate stakeholders are patients, the health research community should actively recruit to its cause not only well-known personalities such as Stephen Cooper, who has made courageous statements about the value of animal research, but all who receive medical treatment.","locateZh":"最后，由于最终的利益相关方是患者，医学研究界应当积极争取支持其事业的人——不仅是像斯蒂芬·库珀这样勇敢公开发声肯定动物研究价值的知名人士，还应包括所有接受过医疗的人。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"a well-known humanist 中 well-known 来自原文 well-known personalities such as Stephen Cooper，但 humanist（人文主义者/人道主义者）在原文完全找不到对应；原文只说他是「知名人物」，并未说他是哪个领域的「人文主义者」。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"a medical practitioner 「医生/医务从业者」在原文找不到任何提示——只说他公开发声支持动物研究，并未提到他是不是医生。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"an enthusiast in animal rights 「动物权利的热心人」恰好与原文立场相反——库珀公开支持动物研究，而动物权利倡导者正是反对动物研究的一方。立场极性颠倒。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"定语从句给出的立场词 value 与 courageous statements 共同指向「公开支持」，与 a supporter of animal research 完全对应。","pairs":[{"orig":"who has made courageous statements about the value of animal research","opt":"a supporter of animal research"}]}}}}}]},{"id":"section2-part-a-3","kind":"reading","title":"Text 3","textNo":3,"directions":"","points":10,"passage":{"title":"Text 3","paragraphs":[{"id":"p-section2-part-a-3-1","text":"In recent years, railroads have been combining with each other, merging into supersystems, causing heightened concerns about monopoly. As recently as 1995, the top four railroads accounted for under 70 percent of the total ton-miles moved by rails. Next year, after a series of mergers is completed, just four railroads will control well over 90 percent of all the freight moved by major rail carriers."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-3-2","text":"Supporters of the new supersystems argue that these mergers will allow for substantial cost reductions and better coordinated service. Any threat of monopoly, they argue, is removed by fierce competition from trucks. But many shippers complain that for heavy bulk commodities traveling long distances, such as coal, chemicals, and grain, trucking is too costly and the railroads therefore have them by the throat."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-3-3","text":"The vast consolidation within the rail industry means that most shippers are served by only one rail company. Railroads typically charge such \"captive\" shippers 20 to 30 percent more than they do when another railroad is competing for the business. Shippers who feel they are being overcharged have the right to appeal to the federal government's Surface Transportation Board for rate relief, but the process is expensive, time consuming, and will work only in truly extreme cases."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-3-4","text":"Railroads justify rate discrimination against captive shippers on the grounds that in the long run it reduces everyone's cost. If railroads charged all customers the same average rate, they argue, shippers who have the option of switching to trucks or other forms of transportation would do so, leaving remaining customers to shoulder the cost of keeping up the line. It's a theory to which many economists subscribe, but in practice it often leaves railroads in the position of determining which companies will flourish and which will fail. \"Do we really want railroads to be the arbiters of who wins and who loses in the marketplace?\" asks Martin Bercovici, a Washington lawyer who frequently represents shippers."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-3-5","text":"Many captive shippers also worry they will soon be hit with a round of huge rate increases. The railroad industry as a whole, despite its brightening fortunes, still does not earn enough to cover the cost of the capital it must invest to keep up with its surging traffic. Yet railroads continue to borrow billions to acquire one another, with Wall Street cheering them on. Consider the $10.2 billion bid by Norfolk Southern and CSX to acquire Conrail this year. Conrail's net railway operating income in 1996 was just $427 million, less than half of the carrying costs of the transaction. Who's going to pay for the rest of the bill? Many captive shippers fear that they will, as Norfolk Southern and CSX increase their grip on the market."}]},"questions":[{"n":31,"stem":"According to those who support mergers, railway monopoly is unlikely because____.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"cost reduction is based on competition."},{"key":"B","text":"services call for cross-trade coordination."},{"key":"C","text":"outside competitors will continue to exist."},{"key":"D","text":"shippers will have the railway by the throat."}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"细节题","stemZh":"根据支持合并的人的观点,铁路垄断不太可能出现,因为____。","choicesZh":{"A":"成本削减是建立在竞争基础上的。","B":"服务需要跨行业协调。","C":"外部竞争者将继续存在。","D":"托运人将扼住铁路的咽喉。"},"analysis":{"type":"According to those who support mergers, railway monopoly is unlikely because 是\"按某人观点 + because\"句式，答案在原文里有原句可对，不需推理。","parse":"主句 railway monopoly is unlikely 是 \"主-系-表\" 结构，because 引出原因状语从句、留空待补；句首 According to those who support mergers 是介词短语作整句状语，限定下文 because 之后的内容必须是\"支持者一方\"给出的理由——一旦把这层归属吃丢，就会把反对方的话当成支持方的话写进答案。","locateEn":"Any threat of monopoly, they argue, is removed by fierce competition from trucks.","locateZh":"他们辩称，垄断的任何威胁都已被来自卡车的激烈竞争所消除。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"支持方原文是\"竞争消除垄断威胁、并因此节省成本\"，cost reduction is based on competition 把\"竞争 → 反垄断\"偷偷换成\"成本下降以竞争为基础\"——支持方的论点是\"为什么不会垄断\"，不是\"成本下降靠什么\"，答非所问。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"支持方理由是降本 + 协调 + 卡车竞争三项并列，services call for cross-trade coordination 只抓住协调这一项，且把\"兼并能带来更好的协调\"扭成\"服务需要跨业协调\"——这一项即便正确，也不是\"为什么垄断不会发生\"的理由，而是兼并的好处。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"预判要的是\"还有铁路之外的对手在\"，C 项 outside competitors will continue to exist 字面就是这一意思——把 trucks 上位为 outside competitors、把 fierce competition 转写为 will continue to exist，与定位句一一对应。","pairs":[{"orig":"fierce competition from trucks","opt":"outside competitors will continue to exist"}]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"shippers will have the railway by the throat 把第 2 段后半句 the railroads therefore have them by the throat 颠倒过来——原文是\"铁路扼住货主咽喉\"且出自反对派之口，与支持方立场无关；既错主体又错归属。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":32,"stem":"What is many captive shippers' attitude towards the consolidation in the rail industry?","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Indifferent."},{"key":"B","text":"Supportive."},{"key":"C","text":"Indignant."},{"key":"D","text":"Apprehensive."}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"态度题","stemZh":"许多受制托运人对铁路行业的合并持什么态度?","choicesZh":{"A":"漠不关心。","B":"支持。","C":"愤慨。","D":"忧虑。"},"analysis":{"type":"What is many captive shippers’ attitude 直接以 attitude 收口，要的是货主对兼并的整体观感，不是某条事实。","parse":"题干 What is many captive shippers’ attitude towards the consolidation in the rail industry? 是 What is X’s attitude towards Y 的特殊疑问句，主语补足语 attitude 是答案落点；towards 引出态度对象 consolidation。注意所有格 shippers’ 划定态度主体——是货主对兼并的看法，不是支持者对兼并的看法，别把对象搞反。","locateEn":"","locateZh":"","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"Indifferent 意为\"漠不关心\"，但原文货主反复用 complain、worry、fear，情绪强烈、绝非漠然。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"Supportive 意为\"支持的\"，但货主明确 complain、worry、fear，是反方而非支持方；与全文立场反向。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"Indignant 意为\"愤怒、义愤填膺\"，强度比原文 worry/fear 高一档；原文情绪集中在\"担心未来涨价\"而非\"愤怒声讨\"，且 complain 也只是\"抱怨\"而非\"愤慨\"。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"预判要的是\"忧虑型负面\"，D 项 Apprehensive 在极性（负）和强度（中度焦虑、非剧烈愤怒）两个维度都精准匹配。","pairs":[{"orig":"Many captive shippers also worry they will soon be hit with a round of huge rate increases","opt":"Apprehensive"},{"orig":"Many captive shippers fear that they will, as Norfolk Southern and CSX increase their grip on the market","opt":"Apprehensive"}]}}}}},{"n":33,"stem":"It can be inferred from paragraph 3 that____.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"shippers will be charged less without a rival railroad."},{"key":"B","text":"there will soon be only one railroad company nationwide."},{"key":"C","text":"overcharged shippers are unlikely to appeal for rate relief."},{"key":"D","text":"a government board ensures fair play in railway business."}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"推断题","stemZh":"从第3段可以推断出____。","choicesZh":{"A":"没有竞争铁路公司时,托运人将被收取更少费用。","B":"全国范围内很快将只有一家铁路公司。","C":"被过度收费的托运人不太可能申诉要求费率减免。","D":"政府委员会确保铁路业务的公平竞争。"},"analysis":{"type":"It can be inferred from paragraph 3 that 明确要求基于第 3 段做推断。","parse":"题干 It can be inferred from paragraph 3 that 用形式主语 it + 不定式被动 to be inferred，from paragraph 3 限定推理材料只能取自第 3 段，that 后接待补完整句。结构上无陷阱，但 from paragraph 3 是硬边界，越界即偏题。","locateEn":"Shippers who feel they are being overcharged have the right to appeal to the federal government's Surface Transportation Board for rate relief, but the process is expensive, time consuming, and will work only in truly extreme cases.","locateZh":"感到被多收钱的货主有权向联邦地面运输委员会申诉以争取费率减免，但这一流程代价高昂、耗时漫长，且只有在真正极端的情形下才会奏效。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"原文 Railroads typically charge such \"captive\" shippers 20 to 30 percent more than they do when another railroad is competing for the business 说\"没有竞争对手时多收 20-30%\"，shippers will be charged less without a rival railroad 恰好反过来——\"没有对手反而少收\"。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"原文 most shippers are served by only one rail company 只说大多数货主对接一家铁路，并未说全国只剩一家铁路；there will soon be only one railroad company nationwide 从\"个体层面只对接一家\"跳到\"国家层面只剩一家\"，跨了好几步，且加了 soon 这种未来时间词，全无原文支撑。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"预判说\"现实里多半不会去申诉\"，C 项 unlikely to appeal for rate relief 字面相符且未越界——正是从\"申诉不划算\"事实出发的一步推论。","pairs":[{"orig":"the process is expensive, time consuming, and will work only in truly extreme cases","opt":"overcharged shippers are unlikely to appeal for rate relief"},{"orig":"Shippers who feel they are being overcharged","opt":"overcharged shippers"}]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"原文对联邦委员会的描述是 expensive, time consuming, and will work only in truly extreme cases，整体语气是\"申诉机制基本无效\"；a government board ensures fair play in railway business 反过来夸它\"保障公平\"，立场反转。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":34,"stem":"The word \"arbiters\"(line 7, paragraph 4) most probably refers to those____.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"who work as coordinators."},{"key":"B","text":"who function as judges."},{"key":"C","text":"who supervise transactions."},{"key":"D","text":"who determine the price."}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"词义句意题","stemZh":"第4段第7行中的\"arbiters\"一词最有可能指那些____的人。","choicesZh":{"A":"担任协调者。","B":"发挥裁判作用。","C":"监督交易。","D":"决定价格。"},"analysis":{"type":"The word \"arbiters\" … most probably refers to 是典型词义题问法。","parse":"题干 The word \"arbiters\"(line 7, paragraph 4) most probably refers to those 主干是 The word refers to those，括号内 line 7, paragraph 4 是位置插入语，most probably 是程度副词、提示\"语境最可能的义项\"而非词典义；those 后接四个 who 从句作进一步限定，因此选项实际比较的是 who 后那个动词的语义。","locateEn":"but in practice it often leaves railroads in the position of determining which companies will flourish and which will fail. \"Do we really want railroads to be the arbiters of who wins and who loses in the marketplace?\" asks Martin Bercovici, a Washington lawyer who frequently represents shippers.","locateZh":"但在实践中，这往往使铁路处于决定哪些公司兴盛、哪些公司倒闭的位置上。\"我们真的希望铁路成为市场上谁胜谁负的裁判吗？\"华盛顿律师马丁·贝尔科维奇问道，他经常代表货主出庭。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"who work as coordinators 取 coordinator（协调者）这一身份，与定位句的\"裁定胜负\"无关；coordinator 是\"调和各方\"，arbiters 是\"判定胜负\"，二者关系上像，但权力性质不同。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"预判要的是\"裁定胜负者\"，B 项 who function as judges 一字命中。","pairs":[{"orig":"determining which companies will flourish and which will fail","opt":"who function as judges"},{"orig":"the arbiters of who wins and who loses in the marketplace","opt":"judges"}]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"who supervise transactions 把 arbiters 解为\"监督交易者\"，但定位句强调的是\"决定 who wins and who loses\"——是裁断者，不是监督者；supervise 是过程性监管，与\"判生死\"语义场不重合。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"who determine the price 抓住 determine 一词与原文 determining 同根，但把判定对象从\"公司兴衰\"缩到了\"价格\"——铁路差别定价确实会决定公司命运，但 arbiter 指代的是\"决定生死\"的总功能，不能缩窄为\"决定价格\"。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":35,"stem":"According to the text, the cost increase in the rail industry is mainly caused by____.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"the continuing acquisition."},{"key":"B","text":"the growing traffic."},{"key":"C","text":"the cheering Wall Street."},{"key":"D","text":"the shrinking market."}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"细节题","stemZh":"根据本文,铁路行业成本增加的主要原因是____。","choicesZh":{"A":"持续的收购。","B":"不断增长的运输量。","C":"华尔街的欢呼。","D":"萎缩的市场。"},"analysis":{"type":"According to the text, the cost increase in the rail industry is mainly caused by 要求按原文复述成本上升的主因，是 According to + caused by 的因果细节题。","parse":"题干 the cost increase in the rail industry is mainly caused by 是被动语态加 by 短语留空，主语 the cost increase 是\"待解释结果\"，by 后面要填的是\"主因\"，mainly 是限定副词、压住选项只能是主因；考生若把 mainly 读漏，看到任何沾边因素都会觉得对。","locateEn":"The railroad industry as a whole, despite its brightening fortunes, still does not earn enough to cover the cost of the capital it must invest to keep up with its surging traffic. Yet railroads continue to borrow billions to acquire one another, with Wall Street cheering them on.","locateZh":"尽管前景趋好，铁路行业整体上仍赚不够钱来覆盖它必须投入、用以跟上激增运量的资本成本。然而铁路公司却继续借入数十亿美元相互收购，华尔街还在一旁鼓劲。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"预判要的是\"持续相互并购\"，A 项 the continuing acquisition 字面对应；同时排除 B 的 surging traffic（背景而非主因）后，A 是唯一精准命中转折后主因的选项。","pairs":[{"orig":"railroads continue to borrow billions to acquire one another","opt":"the continuing acquisition"},{"orig":"Conrail's net railway operating income in 1996 was just $427 million, less than half of the carrying costs of the transaction","opt":"acquisition"}]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"the growing traffic 出自 surging traffic，原文位置在 Yet 转折之前——是\"需要投入资本\"的客观背景，而不是\"成本被进一步推高\"的主因；mainly 限定下，被 Yet 后的并购挤掉。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"the cheering Wall Street 取自 with Wall Street cheering them on——华尔街只是在旁鼓劲，是并购行为的助推者，而不是\"成本上升\"的直接来源；并购本身才是直接花钱方。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"原文 its surging traffic 显示运量在增长、不是萎缩；the shrinking market 直接反向。","pairs":[]}}}}}]},{"id":"section2-part-a-4","kind":"reading","title":"Text 4","textNo":4,"directions":"","points":10,"passage":{"title":"Text 4","paragraphs":[{"id":"p-section2-part-a-4-1","text":"It is said that in England death is pressing, in Canada inevitable and in California optional. Small wonder. Americans' life expectancy has nearly doubled over the past century. Failing hips can be replaced, clinical depression controlled, cataracts removed in a 30-minute surgical procedure. Such advances offer the aging population a quality of life that was unimaginable when I entered medicine 50 years ago. But not even a great health-care system can cure death—and our failure to confront that reality now threatens this greatness of ours."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-4-2","text":"Death is normal; we are genetically programmed to disintegrate and perish, even under ideal conditions. We all understand that at some level, yet as medical consumers we treat death as a problem to be solved. Shielded by third-party payers from the cost of our care, we demand everything that can possibly be done for us, even if it's useless. The most obvious example is late-stage cancer care. Physicians—frustrated by their inability to cure the disease and fearing loss of hope in the patient—too often offer aggressive treatment far beyond what is scientifically justified."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-4-3","text":"In 1950, the US spent $12.7 billion on health care. In 2002, the cost will be $1,540 billion. Anyone can see this trend is unsustainable. Yet few seem willing to try to reverse it. Some scholars conclude that a government with finite resources should simply stop paying for medical care that sustains life beyond a certain age—say 83 or so. Former Colorado governor Richard Lamm has been quoted as saying that the old and infirm \"have a duty to die and get out of the way\", so that younger, healthier people can realize their potential."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-4-4","text":"I would not go that far. Energetic people now routinely work through their 60s and beyond, and remain dazzlingly productive. At 78, Viacom chairman Sumner Redstone jokingly claims to be 53. Supreme Court Justice Sandra Day O'Connor is in her 70s, and former surgeon general C. Everett Koop chairs an Internet start-up in his 80s. These leaders are living proof that prevention works and that we can manage the health problems that come naturally with age. As a mere 68-year-old, I wish to age as productively as they have."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-4-5","text":"Yet there are limits to what a society can spend in this pursuit. As a physician, I know the most costly and dramatic measures may be ineffective and painful. I also know that people in Japan and Sweden, countries that spend far less on medical care, have achieved longer, healthier lives than we have. As a nation, we may be overfunding the quest for unlikely cures while underfunding research on humbler therapies that could improve people's lives."}]},"questions":[{"n":36,"stem":"What is implied in the first sentence?","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Americans are better prepared for death than other people."},{"key":"B","text":"Americans enjoy a higher life quality than ever before."},{"key":"C","text":"Americans are over-confident of their medical technology."},{"key":"D","text":"Americans take a vain pride in their long life expectancy."}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"推断题","stemZh":"第一句话暗示了什么?","choicesZh":{"A":"美国人比其他人更好地为死亡做好了准备。","B":"美国人享有比以往更高的生活质量。","C":"美国人对其医疗技术过于自信。","D":"美国人对其较长的预期寿命抱有虚荣的自豪感。"},"analysis":{"type":"题干 What is implied in the first sentence? 明确用 implied 询问言外之意，不是问首句字面陈述了什么。","parse":"What is implied in the first sentence? 是一个被动语态的特殊疑问句，主干是 What is implied，介词短语 in the first sentence 限定 imply 的发生位置；句子直白，无陷阱。","locateEn":"It is said that in England death is pressing, in Canada inevitable and in California optional. Small wonder. Americans' life expectancy has nearly doubled over the past century.","locateZh":"据说在英国死亡是迫近的，在加拿大是不可避免的，在加利福尼亚是可选的。难怪如此。美国人的预期寿命在过去一个世纪里几乎翻了一倍。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"Americans are better prepared for death than other people 说美国人更好地“准备应对死亡”，原文恰恰反着说：美国人把死亡当 optional、并未做好心理准备。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"Americans enjoy a higher life quality than ever before 把首句重心说成“生活质量更高”，这是文中后文 Such advances offer the aging population a quality of life that was unimaginable 才有的意思，且首句本身没说生活质量，而是讽刺死亡观；偷换了首句的话题焦点。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"预判“美国人对医疗能力过度乐观”与 over-confident of their medical technology 在语义上同框，且 over- 前缀完整捕捉了 optional 这一极端化用词背后的讽刺色彩，故锁 C。","pairs":[{"orig":"in California optional","opt":"over-confident of their medical technology"},{"orig":"Small wonder. Americans' life expectancy has nearly doubled","opt":"over-confident"}]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"Americans take a vain pride in their long life expectancy 说美国人为自己的长寿“虚荣自豪”；原文首句根本没提“自豪”或“虚荣”，且寿命翻倍是事实陈述、非情绪渲染，属凭空加情绪。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":37,"stem":"The author uses the example of cancer patients to show that____.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"medical resources are often wasted"},{"key":"B","text":"doctors are helpless against fatal diseases"},{"key":"C","text":"some treatments are too aggressive"},{"key":"D","text":"medical costs are becoming unaffordable"}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"例证目的题","stemZh":"作者使用癌症患者的例子来说明____。","choicesZh":{"A":"医疗资源经常被浪费","B":"医生对致命疾病束手无策","C":"有些治疗过于激进","D":"医疗费用正变得难以承受"},"analysis":{"type":"题干 The author uses the example of cancer patients to show that____. 直接问作者借例子要 show 什么，是教科书式例证目的题问法。","parse":"The author uses the example of cancer patients to show that____. 是主谓宾结构，宾语 the example of cancer patients 被不定式 to show 修饰目的；选项填入 that 引导的宾语从句作 show 的宾语。结构清晰。","locateEn":"Shielded by third-party payers from the cost of our care, we demand everything that can possibly be done for us, even if it's useless. The most obvious example is late-stage cancer care. Physicians—frustrated by their inability to cure the disease and fearing loss of hope in the patient—too often offer aggressive treatment far beyond what is scientifically justified.","locateZh":"由于有第三方付费者为我们承担医疗费用，我们要求一切可能为我们做的事情，哪怕毫无作用。最明显的例子就是晚期癌症医疗。医生——因无法治愈疾病的挫败感和担心病人失去希望——常常给予远超科学依据的激进治疗。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"预判的“医疗资源被过度索取与浪费”与 medical resources are often wasted 在 demand everything…even if useless 与 far beyond what is scientifically justified 两处文本上同时落地，故锁 A。","pairs":[{"orig":"we demand everything that can possibly be done for us, even if it's useless","opt":"medical resources are often wasted"},{"orig":"offer aggressive treatment far beyond what is scientifically justified","opt":"wasted"}]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"doctors are helpless against fatal diseases 描述医生“对绝症无能为力”，原文 Physicians—frustrated by their inability to cure the disease 确有这层意思，但这是例证内部的细节，不是作者举例的“目的”——目的是论证浪费。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"some treatments are too aggressive 与原文 offer aggressive treatment far beyond what is scientifically justified 字面高度重合，但 aggressive 本身只是手段描述，不是论点；作者举癌症例子是要说“资源浪费”，不是单单说“治疗太激进”。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"medical costs are becoming unaffordable 谈“医疗费用难以承受”，这是第三段 Anyone can see this trend is unsustainable 才出现的话题；第二段例证是讲“索取与浪费”，不是讲“承受不起”。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":38,"stem":"The author's attitude toward Richard Lamm's remark is one of____.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"strong disapproval"},{"key":"B","text":"reserved consent"},{"key":"C","text":"slight contempt"},{"key":"D","text":"enthusiastic support"}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"态度题","stemZh":"作者对Richard Lamm言论的态度是____。","choicesZh":{"A":"强烈反对","B":"保留的赞同","C":"轻微的蔑视","D":"热情的支持"},"analysis":{"type":"题干 The author's attitude toward Richard Lamm's remark is one of____. 用 attitude 直接问作者对某人言论的态度，是态度题的标准句式。","parse":"The author's attitude toward Richard Lamm's remark is one of____. 主干 The author's attitude is one of ___，介词短语 toward Richard Lamm's remark 修饰 attitude，限定态度的对象。one of 后接抽象名词。结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。","locateEn":"","locateZh":"","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"strong disapproval 把作者态度推到“强烈反对”。原文 I would not go that far 只是温和的“我不会走那么远”，并没有谴责或否定 Lamm 的核心忧虑；末段作者甚至承认了 Lamm 关心的资源上限问题，因此“强烈反对”过头。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"预判的“有所保留的同意”在 reserved consent 上字字落地：consent 对应作者后文承认资源有限的同向立场，reserved 对应 I would not go that far 的边界保留。","pairs":[{"orig":"I would not go that far","opt":"reserved consent"},{"orig":"there are limits to what a society can spend in this pursuit","opt":"consent"}]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"slight contempt 含轻蔑情绪，但原文作者用的是温和的 would not go that far、并以学术化的资源讨论延续 Lamm 的方向，无任何讥讽、贬抑、嘲弄之意。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"enthusiastic support 是“热情支持”，与 I would not go that far 的边界保留正面冲突——作者明确不愿走 Lamm 那么远，更谈不上热情支持。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":39,"stem":"In contrast to the US, Japan and Sweden are funding their medical care____.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"more flexibly"},{"key":"B","text":"more extravagantly"},{"key":"C","text":"more cautiously"},{"key":"D","text":"more reasonably"}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"细节题","stemZh":"与美国相比,日本和瑞典为其医疗保健提供资金的方式____。","choicesZh":{"A":"更灵活","B":"更奢侈","C":"更谨慎","D":"更合理"},"analysis":{"type":"题干 In contrast to the US, Japan and Sweden are funding their medical care____. 是要求填入一个客观状语副词来描述日瑞的资助方式，属事实比对，不需推理。","parse":"In contrast to the US, Japan and Sweden are funding their medical care____. 句首介词短语 In contrast to the US 作状语；主句 Japan and Sweden are funding their medical care 是现在进行时；空格处填副词修饰 funding。结构清晰。","locateEn":"I also know that people in Japan and Sweden, countries that spend far less on medical care, have achieved longer, healthier lives than we have.","locateZh":"我还知道，日本和瑞典——花在医疗上的钱远比我们少——的人民却获得了比我们更长寿、更健康的生活。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"more flexibly 强调“更灵活”，需要原文有 flexibility/adapt/varies 等支撑；原文只说花得少、效果好，未提资助方式上的灵活与否。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"more extravagantly 是“更奢侈/挥霍”，与原文 spend far less on medical care（花得远少）正相反。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"more cautiously（更谨慎）强调资助过程中的“小心翼翼”，原文只给出“花得少 + 效果好”的事实，未涉及决策态度上的谨慎；且单凭花得少也不能等于谨慎——可能是预算少、可能是体制不同。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"预判“花得更少效果更好 = 更明智、更合理”，与 more reasonably 直接对应；该副词同时覆盖“花的钱合理 + 效果合理”两层语义，故锁 D。","pairs":[{"orig":"spend far less on medical care, have achieved longer, healthier lives","opt":"more reasonably"}]}}}}},{"n":40,"stem":"The text intends to express the idea that____.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"medicine will further prolong people's lives"},{"key":"B","text":"life beyond a certain limit is not worth living"},{"key":"C","text":"death should be accepted as a fact of life"},{"key":"D","text":"excessive demands increase the cost of health care"}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"主旨题","stemZh":"本文旨在表达这样的观点:____。","choicesZh":{"A":"医学将进一步延长人们的寿命","B":"超过一定限度的生命不值得活","C":"死亡应该被接受为生活的事实","D":"过度的需求增加了医疗保健的成本"},"analysis":{"type":"题干 The text intends to express the idea that____. 用 the text intends 询问全文主旨，是主旨题最直接的提问方式。","parse":"The text intends to express the idea that____. 主干 The text intends to express the idea，that 引导同位语从句解释 idea 的具体内容。空格在同位语从句的谓语—宾语位置。结构清晰。","locateEn":"","locateZh":"","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"medicine will further prolong people's lives 说“医学将进一步延长寿命”，正是作者全篇批判的乐观态度——首段讽刺 in California optional、第二段批 we demand everything…even if it's useless、末段说 overfunding the quest for unlikely cures 全在反对该方向。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"life beyond a certain limit is not worth living 说“超过某岁数的生命不值得活”，正是 Lamm 的极端观点；作者在 I would not go that far 明确拒绝这一结论，并不是全文主旨。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"预判的“应把死亡当作生命事实接受”在首段末、第二段开头、末段尾三处同时落地，且能解释中间各段的存在，故锁 C。","pairs":[{"orig":"not even a great health-care system can cure death","opt":"death should be accepted as a fact of life"},{"orig":"we treat death as a problem to be solved","opt":"death should be accepted as a fact of life"},{"orig":"Death is normal; we are genetically programmed to disintegrate and perish","opt":"a fact of life"}]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"excessive demands increase the cost of health care 只概括了第二、第三段对“过度需求与费用上涨”的论述，却未覆盖首段、末段关于“接受死亡 / 资金错配到不太可能的治愈方案”的核心命题，主旨小于全文。","pairs":[]}}}}}]},{"id":"section2-part-b","kind":"translation","title":"Section II Part C 英译汉","directions":"Directions: Read the following text carefully and then translate the underlined segments into Chinese. Write your answers on the ANSWER SHEET. ( 10 points)","points":10,"passage":{"paragraphs":[{"id":"p-section2-part-b-1","text":"Human beings in all times and places think about their world and wonder at their place in it. Humans are thoughtful and creative, possessed of insatiable curiosity. (41) Furthermore, humans have the ability to modify the environment in which they live, thus subjecting all other life forms to their own peculiar ideas and fancies. Therefore, it is important to study humans in all their richness and diversity in a calm and systematic manner, with the hope that the knowledge resulting from such studies can lead humans to a more harmonious way of living with themselves and with all other life forms on this planet Earth."},{"id":"p-section2-part-b-2","text":"\"Anthropology\" derives from the Greek words anthropos \"human\" and logos \"the study of.\" By its very name, anthropology encompasses the study of all humankind."},{"id":"p-section2-part-b-3","text":"Anthropology is one of the social sciences. (42) Social science is that branch of intellectual enquiry which seeks to study humans and their endeavors in the same reasoned, orderly, systematic, and dispassioned manner that natural scientists use for the study of natural phenomena."},{"id":"p-section2-part-b-4","text":"Social science disciplines include geography, economics, political science, psychology, and sociology. Each of these social sciences has a subfield or specialization which lies particularly close to anthropology."},{"id":"p-section2-part-b-5","text":"All the social sciences focus upon the study of humanity. Anthropology is a field-study oriented discipline which makes extensive use of the comparative method in analysis. (43) The emphasis on data gathered first-hand, combined with a cross-cultural perspective brought to the analysis of cultures past and present, makes this study a unique and distinctly important social science."},{"id":"p-section2-part-b-6","text":"Anthropological analyses rest heavily upon the concept of culture. Sir Edward Tylor's formulation of the concept of culture was one of the great intellectual achievements of 19th century science. (44) Tylor defined culture as \"…that complex whole which includes belief, art, morals, law, custom, and any other capabilities and habits acquired by man as a member of society.\" This insight, so profound in its simplicity, opened up an entirely new way of perceiving and understanding human life. Implicit within Tylor's definition is the concept that culture is learned, shared, and patterned behavior."},{"id":"p-section2-part-b-7","text":"(45) Thus, the anthropological concept of \"culture,\" like the concept of \"set\" in mathematics, is an abstract concept which makes possible immense amounts of concrete research and understanding."}]},"sentences":[{"n":41,"en":"Furthermore, humans have the ability to modify the environment in which they live, thus subjecting all other life forms to their own peculiar ideas and fancies.","refZh":"此外，人类还有能力改造其所生活的环境，从而使所有其他生命形式都服从于人类自己独有的想法与幻想。","analysis":"","chunks":[{"en":"Furthermore,","lit":"此外，","role":"（句间衔接副词）","note":"难点： 递进，不是转折 Furthermore, humans have the ability to modify the environment in which they live 中 Furthermore 接续上一句\"人类好奇心无止境\"，是顺承递进——译\"此外\"\"而且\"皆可，不可译成\"然而\"。"},{"en":"humans have the ability to modify the environment","lit":"人类有能力改造环境","role":"（主干 主+谓+宾（带不定式作定语））","note":"难点： modify 在此取\"改造、改变\" humans have the ability to modify the environment 中 modify 不取\"修饰\"这个考生最眼熟的义项，而取\"改变、改造\"——主体考生易栽在熟词僻义上（A2），看到搭配宾语是 environment 就要立刻反应过来。"},{"en":"in which they live,","lit":"他们生活在其中的，","role":"（which 引导的后置定语从句，修饰 environment）","note":"难点： in which = where the environment in which they live 中 in which 等于 where，they 回指 humans；后置定语较短，整体提到 environment 前译成\"他们所生活的环境\"。"},{"en":"thus subjecting all other life forms to their own peculiar ideas and fancies.","lit":"从而使所有其他生命形式服从于他们自己独特的想法与幻想。","role":"（现在分词短语作结果状语）","note":"难点： subject A to B + peculiar 的取义 subjecting all other life forms to their own peculiar ideas and fancies 是固定搭配 subject sth to sth=\"使某物受制于/服从于某物\"，不是\"主题、科目\"；peculiar 在此为中性\"独有的、特有的\"，不译成贬义\"古怪的\"；their 回指 humans；fancies 在此与 ideas 并列，译\"奇想/幻想\"。"}]},{"n":42,"en":"Social science is that branch of intellectual enquiry which seeks to study humans and their endeavors in the same reasoned, orderly, systematic, and dispassioned manner that natural scientists use for the study of natural phenomena.","refZh":"社会科学是知识探索的一个分支，它力图以与自然科学家研究自然现象时相同的那种理性、有序、系统且冷静客观的方式，来研究人类及其行为活动。","analysis":"","chunks":[{"en":"Social science is that branch of intellectual enquiry","lit":"社会科学是知识探索的那一分支","role":"（主干 主＋系＋表）","note":"难点： intellectual enquiry 抽象名词具体化 Social science is that branch of intellectual enquiry 中 intellectual enquiry 直译\"智力的探询\"很别扭，按汉语习惯具体化为\"知识探索 / 学术探索\"；that 在此是限定词\"那个\"，与后面 which 从句构成\"那个……的分支\"。"},{"en":"which seeks to study humans and their endeavors","lit":"它力图研究人类及其行为","role":"（which 引导的定语从句（修饰 branch））","note":"难点： endeavors 熟词僻义＋their 回指 study humans and their endeavors 中 endeavors 不是\"努力\"这一单数熟义，复数形式在此指\"人类所从事的活动 / 行为 / 事业\"；their 回指 humans，译时直接还原成\"人类的\"，避免代词断链。"},{"en":"in the same reasoned, orderly, systematic, and dispassioned manner","lit":"以同样理性的、有序的、系统的和冷静的方式","role":"（方式状语，修饰 study）","note":"难点： 四个并列形容词 + dispassioned 取义 reasoned, orderly, systematic, and dispassioned manner 中四个形容词并列修饰 manner，注意 dispassioned（=dispassionate）不是\"无情\"，而是\"不带感情色彩的、冷静客观的\"——与上文 in a calm and systematic manner 呼应。in the same…manner that… 是\"以与……相同的方式\"，that 引导比较从句。"},{"en":"that natural scientists use for the study of natural phenomena.","lit":"自然科学家用于研究自然现象的","role":"（that 引导的定语从句（修饰 manner））","note":"难点： in the same…that… 比较结构整体看 in the same reasoned, orderly, systematic, and dispassioned manner that natural scientists use for the study of natural phenomena 是 in the same X that Y use… 的整体比较结构，意为\"以与 Y 相同的 X\"；这里把社会科学的研究方式比作自然科学的研究方式，是全句的论点所在。"}]},{"n":43,"en":"The emphasis on data gathered first-hand, combined with a cross-cultural perspective brought to the analysis of cultures past and present, makes this study a unique and distinctly important social science.","refZh":"对第一手收集的数据的重视，再加上将跨文化视角运用于对古今文化的分析，这两点使人类学这一研究成为一门独特而尤为重要的社会科学。","analysis":"","chunks":[{"en":"The emphasis on data gathered first-hand,","lit":"对第一手收集的数据的重视，","role":"（主语＋后置定语（过去分词修饰 data））","note":"难点： gathered first-hand 是过去分词后置定语 The emphasis on data gathered first-hand 中 gathered first-hand 是过去分词短语后置修饰 data，相当于 data which is gathered first-hand；主体考生易把 gathered 看成谓语动词，导致找不到真正的主语 The emphasis。first-hand 译\"第一手\"，是田野调查 (field-study) 的核心特征。"},{"en":"combined with a cross-cultural perspective brought to the analysis of cultures past and present,","lit":"再加上带入到对古今文化分析中的跨文化视角，","role":"（过去分词短语作伴随状语（修饰主语 The emphasis））","note":"难点： combined with ＋ brought 双层过去分词嵌套 combined with a cross-cultural perspective brought to the analysis of cultures past and present 是\"combined with＋名词＋brought to＋名词\"的双层结构：外层 combined with 是过去分词作伴随状语；内层 brought to the analysis 又是过去分词后置修饰 perspective。past and present 后置修饰 cultures，按汉语习惯前移成\"古今文化\"。"},{"en":"makes this study a unique and distinctly important social science.","lit":"使这一研究成为一门独特而格外重要的社会科学。","role":"（主干谓语＋宾语＋宾语补足语）","note":"难点： this study 的跨句指代＋distinctly 取义 makes this study a unique and distinctly important social science 中 this study 回指上一句的 anthropology（见 Anthropology is a field-study oriented discipline which makes extensive use of the comparative method in analysis.），译时可点明\"这一研究/这一学科\"或直接还原为\"人类学这门学科\"。distinctly 不是\"清晰地\"，而是程度副词\"格外、尤其\"，修饰 important；make…a… 是\"使……成为……\"的使役结构。"}]},{"n":44,"en":"Tylor defined culture as \"…that complex whole which includes belief, art, morals, law, custom, and any other capabilities and habits acquired by man as a member of society.\"","refZh":"泰勒把文化定义为：\"……一个复杂的整体，它包括信仰、艺术、道德、法律、习俗，以及作为社会成员的人所习得的任何其他能力与习惯。\"","analysis":"","chunks":[{"en":"Tylor defined culture as","lit":"泰勒把文化定义为","role":"（主干 主＋谓＋宾＋ as 引导的宾语补足语标志）","note":"难点： define A as B 整体记 Tylor defined culture as 是 define A as B 的固定搭配\"把 A 定义为 B\"，不要把 as 单独译成\"作为\"；Tylor 是人名，音译\"泰勒\"，全文统一即可。"},{"en":"\"…that complex whole","lit":"\"……那个复杂的整体","role":"（as 引导的核心名词短语（被定义对象））","note":"难点： complex whole 抽象名词的处理 that complex whole 中 whole 在此作名词\"整体\"，complex 修饰它，整体译\"复杂的整体/复合体\"；that 是限定词，呼应后面 which 从句构成\"那个……的复合体\"。原文前的省略号要保留，提示这是节选自更长定义的中段。"},{"en":"which includes belief, art, morals, law, custom,","lit":"它包括信仰、艺术、道德、法律、习俗，","role":"（which 引导的定语从句（修饰 whole），并列宾语）","note":"难点： 五项并列名词逐个落地 belief, art, morals, law, custom 是五个并列宾语，须逐项译出：信仰、艺术、道德、法律、习俗——任何一项漏译或合并都可能丢分，主体考生易把 morals 与 law 合译成\"道德法律\"，要拆开。"},{"en":"and any other capabilities and habits acquired by man as a member of society.\"","lit":"以及作为社会成员的人所获得的任何其他能力与习惯。\"","role":"（and 并列宾语＋过去分词后置定语＋ as 介词短语）","note":"难点： acquired 后置定语＋ as a member 取义 any other capabilities and habits acquired by man as a member of society 中 acquired by man 是过去分词后置修饰 capabilities and habits（=which are acquired by man）；as a member of society 中 as 取\"作为\"，与 P6 句首的 as 不同；man 在此泛指\"人/人类\"，不是\"男人\"，acquired 落到\"获得/习得\"——与下文 culture is learned, shared, and patterned behavior 中的 learned 呼应。"}]},{"n":45,"en":"Thus, the anthropological concept of \"culture,\" like the concept of \"set\" in mathematics, is an abstract concept which makes possible immense amounts of concrete research and understanding.","refZh":"因此，人类学中\"文化\"这一概念，就像数学中\"集合\"这一概念一样，是一个抽象概念，但它使大量具体的研究与理解成为可能。","analysis":"","chunks":[{"en":"Thus,","lit":"因此，","role":"（句间衔接副词（结论/总结））","note":"难点： Thus 承接上文 Tylor 定义 Implicit within Tylor's definition is the concept that culture is learned, shared, and patterned behavior. 给出了基于泰勒定义引申出的文化观，Thus, the anthropological concept of \"culture,\" 中的 Thus 即承接此句作结论：因此/由此可见，人类学的文化概念……。"},{"en":"the anthropological concept of \"culture,\"","lit":"人类学的\"文化\"概念，","role":"（主语）","note":"难点： the concept of X 框架＋ \"culture\" 加引号 the anthropological concept of \"culture,\" 中 the concept of \"culture\" 是\"……的概念\"框架，译时保留\"文化\"两边的引号——这里的引号是把 culture 当术语提及，与原文一致。anthropological 译\"人类学的\"，与全文术语统一。"},{"en":"like the concept of \"set\" in mathematics,","lit":"就像数学里\"集合\"这个概念一样，","role":"（插入语 / 类比状语）","note":"难点： set 取数学义\"集合\"、like 是类比 like the concept of \"set\" in mathematics 中 set 不是\"放置/一组\"，在 in mathematics 语境下取数学术语\"集合\"——这是典型的熟词僻义；like 在此作介词\"如同/就像\"，整段是插入的类比状语。\"set\" 与 \"culture\" 一样加引号，译文保留。"},{"en":"is an abstract concept","lit":"是一个抽象概念","role":"（主干谓语＋表语）","note":"难点： 主干被插入语隔开 the anthropological concept of \"culture,\" like the concept of \"set\" in mathematics, is an abstract concept 的主干是\"概念……是一个抽象概念\"，被中间 like… 的插入类比状语隔开，翻译时需把插入语前移或后置，让主干 \"……概念是抽象概念\" 不被打断。"},{"en":"which makes possible immense amounts of concrete research and understanding.","lit":"它使大量具体的研究与理解成为可能。","role":"（which 引导的定语从句（修饰 concept），含 make+宾补＋后置宾语的倒装）","note":"难点： makes possible X 是宾补＋宾语倒装 makes possible immense amounts of concrete research and understanding 正常语序应是 makes immense amounts of…possible，因宾语 immense amounts of concrete research and understanding 过长，把宾补 possible 提前——这是\"宾语过长导致宾补前移\"的常见倒装，意为\"使……成为可能\"；不可误读为\"使可能的大量研究\"。abstract↔concrete 在本句形成对照：抽象概念却催生大量具体研究。"}]}]},{"id":"section3","kind":"writing","title":"Section III 写作","directions":"46. Directions: Study the following set of drawings carefully and write an essay entitled in which you should 1）describe the set of drawings, interpret its meaning, and 2）point out its implications in our life. You should write about 200 words neatly on ANSWER SHEET 2. (20 points)","prompt":"46. Directions: Study the following set of drawings carefully and write an essay entitled in which you should 1）describe the set of drawings, interpret its meaning, and 2）point out its implications in our life. You should write about 200 words neatly on ANSWER SHEET 2. (20 points)","points":20,"image":{"src":"assets/writing/2003-section3.jpg","alt":"这是一幅黑白漫画，包含上下两幅图和一个标题","width":"758","height":"657","local":"assets/writing/2003-section3.jpg"},"model":{"essay":"As is vividly depicted in the set of drawings, a delicate flower blossoms safely inside a warm greenhouse, while a storm rages outside the glass. In the second picture, however, the same flower has been moved into the open air, where the wind and rain beat it until its petals and leaves fall to the ground. Simple as the two pictures are, they convey a thought-provoking message about growth. The flower symbolizes young people who are raised in overprotected environments, and the greenhouse stands for the excessive shelter offered by parents and schools. Just as the flower cannot survive once it loses its glass shield, individuals who have never faced hardship easily collapse when real difficulties strike. A child who is always spared from failure, for instance, never learns how to stand up after falling. The drawings therefore remind us that genuine strength is forged through storms, not shelter. For our society, we should encourage young people to step out of their comfort zones and temper themselves in real challenges. Parents and educators, meanwhile, ought to loosen their protective grip and allow the young to experience setbacks. Only in this way can the next generation grow into trees strong enough to weather any wind and rain.","translation":"如这组漫画所生动描绘的，一朵娇嫩的花在温暖的温室里安然绽放，而玻璃外则风雨交加。然而在第二幅图中，同一朵花被移到了露天之下，风雨将它拍打得东倒西歪，直到花瓣与叶子纷纷落地。\n两幅画虽然简单，却传达出一个关于成长的发人深省的信息。花象征着在过度保护的环境中长大的年轻人，温室则象征着父母与学校给予的过度庇护。正如花一旦失去玻璃的护罩便无法存活，从未经历艰难的人，一旦真正的困难来袭便极易崩溃。举例来说，一个总是被免于失败的孩子，永远学不会跌倒之后如何重新站起。\n因此，这组漫画提醒我们：真正的坚强是在风雨中磨炼出来的，而非在庇护中得来的。就社会而言，我们应鼓励年轻人走出舒适区，在真实的挑战中磨炼自己。与此同时，父母与教育者也应松开过度保护的手，允许年轻人经历挫折。唯有如此，下一代才能长成足以抵御任何风雨的大树。","breakdown":[{"tag":"【第 1 段】","core":"客观描述＋前后对比，落实描述任务","text":"【第 1 段】 客观描述＋前后对比，落实描述任务 。本段用\"As is vividly depicted...\"总起，再以\"In the second picture, however...\"交代下图相对上图的转变，两句纯客观描述、不下结论，正是\"客观描述不下结论\"方法的落地，完成 describe the set of drawings。"},{"tag":"【第 2 段】","core":"主题句＋象征对应＋例证撑起寓意","text":"【第 2 段】 主题句＋象征对应＋例证撑起寓意 。以\"Simple as the two pictures are, they convey a thought-provoking message\"亮出寓意主题句，随后\"The flower symbolizes...the greenhouse stands for...\"做象征对应，再用\"A child who...for instance\"举例坐实，三步落实 interpret its meaning。"},{"tag":"【第 3 段】","core":"承上给对策＋倒装升华收尾","text":"【第 3 段】 承上给对策＋倒装升华收尾 。\"The drawings therefore remind us...\"承上升格为提醒，接着分\"young people\"与\"Parents and educators\"两类对象给建议，末句\"Only in this way can...\"倒装收束，回应 point out its implications in our life。"}],"wordCount":206}}],"questionCount":40,"objectiveCount":40,"answerTable":{"ans-section1":"完形填空（四选一） Section I （1–20 题） 1 A thought · 2 B accommodate · 3 C exercise · 4 D Because · 5 C confidence · 6 B admired · 7 D wise · 8 C for example · 9 A displaying · 10 D multiple · 11 A group · 12 D security · 13 B barely · 14 D short · 15 C so that · 16 D something · 17 B down · 18 A On the contrary · 19 C planning · 20 A capability \n（逐题解析见 完形填空（四选一）解析页\n）","ans-section2-part-a-1":"阅读理解（四选一） Section II Part A (1) （21–25 题） 21 B · 22 A · 23 C · 24 D · 25 B \n（逐题解析见 阅读理解（四选一）解析页\n）","ans-section2-part-a-2":"阅读理解（四选一） Section II Part A (2) （26–30 题） 26 A · 27 B · 28 B · 29 A · 30 D \n（逐题解析见 阅读理解（四选一）解析页\n）","ans-section2-part-a-3":"阅读理解（四选一） Section II Part A (3) （31–35 题） 31 C · 32 D · 33 C · 34 B · 35 A \n（逐题解析见 阅读理解（四选一）解析页\n）","ans-section2-part-a-4":"阅读理解（四选一） Section II Part A (4) （36–40 题） 36 C · 37 A · 38 B · 39 D · 40 C \n（逐题解析见 阅读理解（四选一）解析页\n）","ans-section2-part-b":"英译汉（划线句子翻译） Section II Part B \n主观题 · 参考范文与逐题解析见\n 英译汉（划线句子翻译）解析页","ans-section3":"写作 Section III \n主观题 · 参考范文与逐题解析见\n 写作解析页"},"coverage":{"objective":40,"explained":40,"missingSections":[]}};
